    "localNotes": "Georgia's real citizen-petition power is municipal, and it is a charter-amendment power rather than a general ordinance power. Under Ga. Const. art. IX, sec. II and O.C.G.A. sec. 36-35-3 (Municipal Home Rule), voters may petition to amend a municipal charter. Thresholds scale inversely with size, measured against electors registered for the last general municipal election: 25% for municipalities of 5,000 residents or fewer; 20% for municipalities over 5,000 but under 100,000; 15% for municipalities over 100,000. Signatures must be submitted within 60 days of the date the petition sponsor first obtained the petition copies from the clerk of the local governing body. These are high thresholds and a short window by national standards. Approved measures go to a general primary/election or to a statutorily fixed special-election date (in odd years, the third Tuesday in March or the Tuesday after the first Monday in November; in even years, the presidential preference primary date, the third Tuesday in March, the general primary date, or the Tuesday after the first Monday in November). Simple majority to pass. Georgia also allows recall of every elected official.",
