    "localNotes": "Tennessee has no general citizen initiative for local ordinances. There is exactly one citizen-triggered route in the state constitution, and it is indirect. Under Tenn. Const. art. XI, sec. 9, a home rule municipality amends its charter by local referendum, and amendments may be put to that referendum in three ways: (a) by ordinance of the municipal governing body; (b) by a charter commission created by act of the General Assembly and elected by the city's voters; or (c) by a charter commission of seven at-large members chosen in a municipal election held on the petition of not less than 10 percent of the voters of the home rule municipality who voted in the latest general municipal election. Route (c) is the only one citizens control, it may be used no more than once every two years, and MTAS reports it has been used only rarely; route (a) is what cities almost always use. The referendum itself must be held at the first general state election falling at least 60 days after publication of the proposed amendment, which in practice limits home-rule charter referenda to the August primary or November general election in even-numbered years (T.C.A. sec. 6-53-105; notice to the election commission 75-90 days out under T.C.A. sec. 2-3-204). T.C.A. sec. 6-53-105(c) requires the municipal CFO's cost and revenue estimate to appear on the ballot. Only fourteen Tennessee cities have home rule charters: Chattanooga, Clinton, East Ridge, Etowah, Johnson City, Knoxville, Lenoir City, Memphis, Mt. Juliet, Oak Ridge, Red Bank, Sevierville, Sweetwater and Whitwell. Non-home-rule cities are governed by general law or private act charters and have no equivalent citizen petition right.",
