    "localInitiative": true,
    "localNotes": "This is where Texas direct democracy actually lives, and it is the single most important section of this file. There is no statewide initiative, but Texas home-rule municipalities (cities over 5,000 that have adopted a charter) reserve initiative and referendum powers in their own charters, and those powers are unique to home-rule cities: general-law cities, counties and the state have nothing equivalent. Two different instruments, with two different rule sources. (1) CHARTER AMENDMENT, governed by state law and therefore uniform: Tex. Local Gov't Code sec. 9.004 requires the governing body to call an election on a proposed charter amendment when a petition is signed by qualified voters of the municipality equal to at least 5 percent of the number of qualified voters, or 20,000, whichever is smaller. The 20,000 cap is what makes charter amendment the practical tool in the big cities. The election must fall on the next authorized uniform election date, at least 30 days after the ordering ordinance. (2) ORDINANCE INITIATIVE AND REFERENDUM, governed by each city's own charter, so thresholds differ city to city and must be read from the charter itself. Two worked examples: AUSTIN - initiative (propose an ordinance), referendum (repeal one) and charter amendment all require 5 percent of qualified voters of the city or 20,000, whichever is smaller; recall requires 10 percent; a referendum petition must be filed before the ordinance takes effect, typically about 10 business days after adoption; the clerk needs at least 30 days to verify a 20,000-signature petition. HOUSTON - thresholds are keyed to the vote for Mayor rather than to registered voters, and are much higher: initiative 15 percent of the total votes cast for Mayor in 2023 = 37,915 signatures; referendum 10 percent = 25,277; recall 25 percent = 63,192; signatures on an initiative, referendum or recall petition may be no older than 30 days when filed. Houston charter amendment falls back to the state 20,000 figure under sec. 9.004, with a 180-day signature-age limit. The lesson for organizers: in Texas the same campaign can face a 20,000-signature charter route and a 37,915-signature ordinance route in the same city, and picking the wrong instrument is the expensive mistake.",
