    "preFilingReview": "Front-loaded and heavy. File an application with the Lieutenant Governor naming at least five sponsors with notarized signatures, each a Utah resident who has voted in a Utah regular general election within the last three years, plus the full proposed law with a title (Utah Code 20A-7-202). The Lieutenant Governor screens it on the grounds listed above. The Office of the Legislative Fiscal Analyst then has 25 calendar days to issue an initial fiscal impact estimate, which sponsors may challenge within 20 calendar days (20A-7-202.5). Before any signature may be gathered, sponsors must personally hold at least seven public hearings, one in each of seven regions (Bear River, Southwest, Mountain, Central, Southeast, Uintah Basin, Wasatch Front), each at least one hour long with at least one hour for public comment, each noticed at least 10 calendar days in advance, each recorded, and each with the fiscal statement posted and at least 50 copies available (20A-7-204.1). Sponsors may revise the text by addendum within 14 days after the seventh hearing, which triggers a new fiscal estimate. Petition packets must then be assembled in a prescribed order and bound across the top in at least three places before circulation (20A-7-204). Circulators must be 18 or older, must show each signer a printed or digital copy of the proposed law and wait while they read it, may not sign their own packet, and paid circulators must be paid hourly rather than per verified signature and must wear a compliant badge. After qualification, the Office of Legislative Research and General Counsel writes a 125-word ballot statement, and the voter information pamphlet takes a 500-word argument for and a 250-word rebuttal.",
