    "localNotes": "Vermont has no statewide initiative but has one of the strongest local petition rights in the country, and it is the route that matters here. Under 17 V.S.A. sec. 2642(a)(3)(A) the warning for a municipal meeting must contain any article requested by a petition signed by at least five percent of the voters of the municipality and filed with the municipal clerk not less than 47 days before the day of the meeting. Town Meeting Day is the first Tuesday in March (17 V.S.A. sec. 2640(a)); towns may begin their annual meeting on the three preceding days (sec. 2640(b)), but the 47-day clock still runs from the statutory Town Meeting Day, not from the earlier start. The clerk must examine the petition immediately and, if it does not conform, must state in writing why and return it to the petitioners within 24 hours. An article that includes a bonding proposal requires ten percent rather than five percent. Vermont municipalities may also be petitioned for non-binding advisory articles, and the Vermont League of Cities and Towns notes that a selectboard has some discretion to decline purely advisory petitions - so an organizer should draft the article as a binding question within the municipality's legal authority wherever possible.",
