    "preFilingReview": "Two mandatory pre-circulation steps. (1) Code Reviser review: after initial filing (with a $156 fee and a sponsor affidavit, filed online), the Secretary of State transmits the text to the Office of the Code Reviser, which has 7 working days to review for technical errors and style, advise the sponsor of conflicts with existing law, and issue a Certificate of Review. The Code Reviser's suggestions are advisory; the sponsor may accept or reject them. The sponsor then has 15 working days from the initial filing to submit the final text plus the Certificate of Review; after that no further changes are allowed. (2) Ballot title and summary: the Attorney General has 5 working days to issue a 10-word subject statement, a 30-word concise description, and a summary of not more than 75 words; any person may challenge the title or summary in Thurston County Superior Court within 5 working days. Petition sheets may only be printed after the appeal period closes.",
    "localInitiative": true,
    "localNotes": "Local initiative power in Washington is NOT universal; it depends on the city's classification and whether the city has adopted the power. (a) Non-charter code cities (the most common form): the voters or the council may adopt initiative and referendum powers 'in the manner provided for changing the classification of a city or town' (RCW 35A.11.080). Once adopted, RCW 35A.11.100 sets the petition threshold at 'fifteen percent of the total number of names of persons listed as registered voters within the city on the day of the last preceding city general election.' RCW 35A.11.090 makes ordinances in such cities effective only 30 days after passage and subject to referendum during that window, with seven exceptions (including initiated ordinances, emergency measures, LIDs, appropriations, collective bargaining, employee compensation, and tax levies). (b) Commission-form cities have initiative and referendum by statute (ch. 35.17 RCW). (c) First-class charter cities (e.g. Seattle, Spokane, Tacoma) have whatever initiative power their charter grants, and thresholds vary by charter. (d) Counties: only home-rule charter counties (e.g. King, Snohomish, Pierce, Whatcom, Clallam, San Juan) have initiative power, and only as their charter provides. Organizers must check the specific city's classification first.",
