    "citizenRouteNotes": "Qualified yes, and this is Washington's distinguishing feature. For ORDINARY BILLS: the Code Reviser's Office drafts only for legislators, standing committees, the governor, executive agencies, and the courts; a member of the public must find a legislator to sponsor and request the draft. But for a CITIZEN INITIATIVE the Code Reviser's review is built into the statutory process and is available to any registered Washington voter who files an initiative and pays the $156 fee: RCW 29A.72.020 gives the Code Reviser 7 working days to review the sponsor's draft for technical errors and style, to advise the sponsor of conflicts with existing law, and to return the proposal with recommended changes plus a Certificate of Review. The sponsor may accept or reject the recommendations. That means an organizer in Washington can get free, nonpartisan, professional legislative-drafting feedback on their own text without a legislator's involvement — a route that does not exist in most states. A citizen may also bring a bill idea to any legislator; the WA legislative hotline is 1-800-562-6000.",
