    "localNotes": "Wisconsin's DIRECT LEGISLATION statute, s. 9.20 Wis. Stat., is a genuine and underused citizen lawmaking mechanism, and it is the most important thing on this page for an organizer in Wisconsin. Scope: cities and villages only (not towns, not counties, not school districts). Threshold: a number of electors equal to at least 15 percent of the votes cast for governor at the last general election IN THAT CITY OR VILLAGE may sign and file a petition with the city or village clerk attaching a proposed ordinance or resolution. Because the base is the municipality's own gubernatorial vote, the absolute number is small in most Wisconsin municipalities — this is a low-cost mechanism relative to a statewide campaign. Signature window: no signature is valid unless dated less than 60 DAYS before the date offered for filing (s. 9.20(2m)) — so the entire petition must be gathered inside a rolling 60-day window, which is the tightest constraint. After filing, no name may be erased or removed. Petition form is governed by s. 8.40. Clerk review: within 15 days the clerk must determine whether the petition is sufficient and the proposed ordinance is in proper form, and must state findings in a signed, dated certificate; if insufficient, the petition may be amended or the ordinance put in proper form within 10 days of the original certificate. Council action: the common council or village board must, WITHOUT ALTERATION, either pass the ordinance within 30 days of the clerk's final certificate, or submit it to the electors at the next spring or general election (if that election is more than 70 days out; otherwise the next election after). A three-fourths vote of the members-elect can order a special election, but no more than one direct-legislation special election in any 6-month period. Passage: majority of those voting; the ordinance takes effect on publication, which must occur within 10 days after the election. Protection: an ordinance adopted under s. 9.20 is NOT subject to the mayor's veto and may NOT be repealed or amended within 2 years of adoption except by a vote of the electors (s. 9.20(8)). JUDICIAL LIMITS matter here and campaigns have died on them: the courts have held that (a) a proposal that is administrative rather than legislative in character is not a proper subject of initiative (State ex rel. Becker v. Common Council, 101 Wis. 2d 680 (Ct. App. 1981)); (b) the power does not extend to legislative decisions already made by the body (Schaefer v. Village Board, 177 Wis. 2d 287 (Ct. App. 1993)); (c) where statutes establish procedures for legislating in an area, an initiative may not modify those statutory directives — s. 62.23 does this for zoning, so ZONING MAY NOT BE LEGISLATED OR MODIFIED BY INITIATIVE, and an ordinance constituting a pervasive regulation of or prohibition on the use of land is zoning (Heitman v. City of Mauston Common Council, 226 Wis. 2d 542 (Ct. App. 1999)); and (d) an initiative may not compel repeal of an existing ordinance or passage of an ordinance in clear conflict with existing ordinances, nor exercise legislative powers not conferred on the municipality. But a council has NO authority to make an initial judgment on the constitutionality or validity of proposed direct legislation (State ex rel. Althouse v. City of Madison, 79 Wis. 2d 97 (1977)), and the clerk has a mandatory duty to forward a sufficient petition in proper form to the council (State ex rel. North v. Goetz, 116 Wis. 2d 239 (Ct. App. 1983)).",
