    "localNotes": "West Virginia provides no general municipal or county initiative or referendum on ordinances. W. Va. Code ch. 8, art. 11 (Powers and Duties With Respect to Ordinances and Ordinance Procedures, sections 8-11-1 through 8-11-5) contains no citizen petition mechanism to enact or repeal a municipal ordinance. What West Virginia residents DO have at the local level is a charter petition route, which is slower but can achieve structural change: (a) FRAMING A NEW CHARTER — under W. Va. Code sec. 8-4-1 the governing body of a city must submit the charter question to the voters upon a petition signed in the signers' own handwriting by 15 percent of the qualified voters of the city (Class I or Class II city) or 10 percent (Class III city); if the petition is presented more than 120 days before the next general or regular municipal election and requests a special election, the governing body must call one to be held not less than 30 nor more than 60 days after presentation. (b) REVISING OR AMENDING AN EXISTING CHARTER — under W. Va. Code sec. 8-4-7 qualified voters may amend a charter on the same 15%/10% petition thresholds, filed more than 120 days before the next regular municipal election to trigger a special municipal election; a charter may not be revised as a whole within four years of its effective date or last revision, and may not be amended within one year of its effective date or last revision. Sufficiency of any petition filed under ch. 8 is determined under sec. 8-5-18. Because charters can allocate powers and create or restructure municipal bodies, a charter amendment petition is the closest thing West Virginia has to citizen-initiated local lawmaking. Individual home-rule municipalities may also have charter-based initiative or referendum provisions of their own; check the specific city charter.",
